# Proposal: Local Biomedical Image Review Workspace (working name "Slidebook")

Stage: research (frozen). Companion catalogs: `sources.json` (S##), `witnesses.json` (W##), `leads.json` (L##).
Labels used throughout: **[FACT]** external, source-backed; **[INFERENCE]** engineering conclusion from facts; **[CHOICE]** product decision we are free to change; **[VALIDATION-EXECUTED]** check run by this candidate with receipt; **[VALIDATION-PROPOSED]** check specified but UNEXECUTED.

---

## 0. Executive summary

Build the workspace as a **project directory bundle** (versioned JSON manifest + per-source sidecars + annotation store + provenance log) around a **strictly separated core: immutable source identity, explicit coordinate frames, display state kept out of data, and derived artifacts as recorded operations**. Two independently useful precedents anchor the design: **napari** contributes the nD layer/coordinate model and the axis-order lesson (axis names, right-aligned negative indexing, per-layer display state) [S15][S16]; **QuPath** contributes the project/persistence model (JSON project file, per-entry data directories, per-machine path variants) and an explicit "what the export preserves" contract for annotation export [S19][S20][S21][S22]. Chunked bounded access follows **Zarr/OME-NGFF** storage conventions [S17].

One complete issue→fix→regression-test→release chain is documented and pinned: **zarr-python issue #3411 → PR #3412 → first shipped in release v3.1.3** (absent at v3.1.0–v3.1.2, present at v3.1.3+; PyPI upload dates 2025-08-25 vs 2025-09-18) [S02][S03][S04][S05–S13]. Its lessons directly shape our atomic-save design (§7).

The 3 GB bounded-access target is an engineering target with a proposed benchmark, not a demonstrated property of any component we cite [VALIDATION-PROPOSED P1]. No full application is built in this experiment.

## 1. Scope and product boundary

- **[CHOICE]** Single-user desktop tool, one project open at a time, exchanged as directory bundles over ordinary shared storage. No cloud, no live co-editing, no DICOM, no vendor-proprietary reader support in v1 (see §9 support boundary).
- **[CHOICE]** Research review tool: annotations are review records, not clinical findings; no diagnostic claims anywhere in the UI.
- **[FACT]** Precedents we reuse are small components, not whole products: we do not claim napari or QuPath behave as this proposal describes beyond the cited pages/files [S15–S22].

## 2. Minimum workflow, mapped to design obligations

| Brief obligation | Design response (§) |
|---|---|
| create project | manifest-first bundle; empty project is valid (§4, §5) |
| import local data without rewriting originals | readers open sources read-only; identity = content hash; copies only on explicit user action (§6) |
| inspect metadata & provenance | provenance panel renders the import record verbatim + corrections overlay (§6.3) |
| navigate & adjust display | display state (visible channels, window/level, slice/time) stored under `view`, never written to sample data (§5.3) |
| annotate points/polygons/masks; plane- vs volume-scoped | annotation schema with explicit frame-of-reference binding (§5.2) |
| visible/stored/physical coordinate relationship | coordinate frames: named axes + index→physical affine per source (§5.1) |
| save / close / reopen on another machine | generation-numbered atomic saves; per-machine path table (§7, §8) |
| export annotation table/overlay + small derived image | export manifest recording what is preserved and what is not (§10) |
| transform/resample is a distinct recorded operation | derived-artifact records (§6.4) |
| unsupported/ambiguous axes or calibration | import validation gate; explicit correction choice; original metadata retained (§6.3, §9) |
| 3 GB image, bounded memory | chunked reader + bounded tile cache; target + benchmark (§11) |
| cancel long operations, progress, recoverable failures | cooperative cancel tokens; temp+rename saves; generation rollback (§7) |

## 3. Evidence base (what was discovered, and its limits)

**[FACT]** All external claims below are backed by the captures pinned in `sources.json`. Two capture limits must be stated up front: the NGFF 0.5 spec page capture is the first 32,768 of 129,734 bytes (claims restricted to the axes/storage sections visible in the capture) [S17], and GitHub REST API access was rate-limited mid-session, so commit metadata comes from the PR `.patch`/`.diff` endpoints and tag-level raw-file fetches instead of the commits API [S23].

### 3.1 Precedent 1 — napari: nD layer model and axis-order lesson (independent mechanism: viewer/coordinate model)

- **[FACT]** napari describes itself as a viewer for 2D/higher-dimensional arrays with overlays for points/polygons/segmentations and annotation/editing of derived datasets [S16].
- **[FACT]** The napari "Axis names" guide documents a real repair history for axis-order confusion: before 0.7.0 axis labels were positional and "the mismatch between the layer axes and the viewer axes can result in confusing UI and subtle bugs"; napari 0.7.0 adopted negative (right-aligned) indexing for default axis labels so mixed-dimensionality layers align like NumPy broadcasting; axis *names/units* then gained gradual automatic propagation (units in 0.7.x, names in 0.9.x) [S15].
- **Mechanism contributed [INFERENCE]:** mixed-dimensional layers align by *right-aligned named axes*, and every layer carries its own display state (visibility, contrast, colormap) and optional affine transform. We adopt right-aligned named axes as the project-wide coordinate convention (§5.1). This is independent of QuPath's mechanism (below): napari contributes *how coordinates and display state relate to data at view time*; it has no multi-file project persistence model of its own in the cited sources.
- Independence justification: napari's docs/spec we cite contain no project-bundle or export-table design; QuPath's contain no nD viewer coordinate model. Each can be adopted without the other.

### 3.2 Precedent 2 — QuPath: project persistence, annotation object model, honest export contract (independent mechanism: storage/annotation model)

- **[FACT]** A QuPath project is a directory: a JSON `project.qpproj` file whose entries hold a `serverBuilder` (reader library + URI), a unique `entryID`, and a human-readable `imageName`; per-entry subdirectories under `data/` hold `data.qpdata`, `thumbnail.jpg`, and `summary.json` (written together with the data, to preview without opening the large file) [S19].
- **[FACT]** QuPath documents the external-path relocation pattern: duplicate `.qpproj` files in one project directory may define *different image paths per machine*, "the paths to the images might differ for each computer", with a warning that adding images later under duplicates can create different images with the same IDs [S19].
- **[FACT]** QuPath's annotation objects have name, ROI, classification, measurement list; annotations are flexible/manual (hundreds per image) while detections are efficient (millions) [S21]. All shape exports use **pixel units with origin at the top-left of the full-resolution image**, "may be different from how other software expects the origin and units"; GeoJSON export notes ellipses become polygons; labeled vs multi-channel binary export have explicitly documented semantics including draw-order collisions [S20].
- **[FACT]** QuPath's image concept docs separate display from data: brightness/contrast changes the LUT "without changing the underlying pixel values"; "the pixel values are the raw data"; pixel size metadata is "not guaranteed to be a) present, or b) correct"; magnification is scanner-dependent; z-stack/time-series support is limited and "Objects (e.g. annotations, cells) also should remember which plane they belong to" [S22].
- **Mechanism contributed [INFERENCE]:** (a) project = manifest + per-entry directories with small summaries written alongside large data; (b) per-machine path variants with identity kept by entry ID (we strengthen this with content hashes, §8); (c) an export page that states coordinate units/origin and lossy mappings in plain language — our export manifest copies this honesty duty (§10).

### 3.3 Supporting component — Zarr / OME-NGFF storage conventions

- **[FACT]** OME-NGFF 0.5 stores bioimaging data on Zarr v3; "axes" metadata requires unique `name`, should carry `type` ("space"|"time"|"channel") and UDUNITS-2 `unit`; `dimension_names` MUST be present on each array and match the axes; the layout bounds dimensions to 2–5 with time before channel before spatial axes; labels ride alongside images in a `labels` group [S17].
- **[INFERENCE]** We adopt the axes dictionary shape (name/type/unit) for our coordinate frames even for plain TIFF imports, so every source in the project has identical metadata shape regardless of origin. Full OME-NGFF compliance is a deferred opportunity (L6), not a v1 obligation.

### 3.4 Issue→fix→regression chain (complete, pinned): zarr-python atomic writes

- **Issue:** zarr-python **#3411 "Atomic writes in LocalStore"**, opened 2025-08-27 by Stephan Hoyer: Zarr v2's `DirectoryStore` used atomic rename-into-place writes (permalink at commit `f1978dde52b11a7803a1a06937e69948acc8fe84`, `zarr/storage.py` lines ~1146–1161), but Zarr v3's `LocalStore` "just seems to be using Python's file interface (via `open`)" (permalink at commit `c9509ee4416499e0b9bf735acb0820d989a61ebe`, `src/zarr/storage/_local.py` lines ~52–55); "Per @rabernat, this was almost certainly not intentional" [S02].
- **Fix:** **PR #3412 "Use atomic writes for new files in LocalStore"** ("Fixes #3411"), merged 2025-08-28T17:41:27Z. The diff adds `_atomic_write`: write to `path.with_suffix(f".{uuid4().hex}.partial")`, then `tmp_path.replace(path)` (non-exclusive) or `_safe_move` (`os.link`+`os.unlink` on Unix to get FileExistsError semantics, `os.rename` on Windows), unlinking the temp on exception; a towncrier fragment `changes/3411.bugfix.rst` reads "LocalStore now uses atomic writes, which should prevent some cases of corrupted data." Four regression tests are added in `tests/test_store/test_local.py`, including `test_atomic_write_incomplete`, which raises mid-write and asserts the target does **not** exist and no temp files remain [S03]. The PR is 6 commits; the last branch commit is `f2c1fcd45562327fa970541cb0c846f83c481406` ("Add release note") [S04].
- **Release applicability (verified by tag-level file checks, not inferred from dates):** `_atomic_write` is **absent** in `src/zarr/storage/_local.py` at tags `v3.1.0` [S05], `v3.1.1` [S06], `v3.1.2` [S07] and **present** at `v3.1.3` [S08] and `v3.2.0` [S09]; the four regression tests are absent at `v3.1.0` [S11] and present at `v3.1.3` [S10]. PyPI uploads: 3.1.2 on 2025-08-25T15:32:29Z (before the PR) and 3.1.3 on 2025-09-18T19:32:40Z (after) [S12][S13]. **First release containing the fix: v3.1.3.**
- **Corollary discovered during tag probing [FACT]:** at v3.1.3, `LocalStore` also lost `supports_partial_writes` and `_put` lost its `start` parameter — partial in-place writes were *removed* rather than made atomic [S08][S09]; PR #3412's body already questioned whether atomic and efficient partial writes can coexist, linking discussion #2859 [S03].
- **Limits of this chain (honesty):** it establishes a *library-level* fix. The regression tests cover the `_atomic_write` helper (success, interrupted, and pre-existing-target cases) but not concurrent writers, power loss, or network filesystems; the PR text itself flags partial writes as unresolved. I did not capture the merge commit SHA on `main` (GitHub API rate limit) [S23]; applicability is pinned by tag-file content plus PyPI dates instead, which is stronger evidence for "which release has it" than a merge SHA would be. None of this demonstrates any behavior of a full application using zarr.

